  "claims": [
    "Eleven directed pure-wire local forms with four context placements are sound; the two associator inverse cancellation forms repair an omission in the older nine-form language. Completeness and termination of these forms are not established.",
    "One mixed catalogue now admits actual wire edits, wire/circuit sequential and tensor fusion, arbitrary mixed association, and MAC contraction; its optional suffix contraction is a local completion.",
    "Without the suffix completion, association and MAC contraction have distinct forward-irreducible descendants after either copy or discard, even with all eleven wire forms and both fusion rules available. The catalogue does not satisfy the earlier RootRigid hypothesis.",
    "Every completed-catalogue primitive, contextual step, and reversible proof track expands into an old-catalogue proof track with exactly the same finite requirement set. The expansion of suffix contraction is inverse association then contextual MAC contraction.",
    "Any checked model of the uncompleted catalogue extends to the completed catalogue without new local semantic assumptions, using the expanded old proof.",
    "A single typed parameterized suffix-definition generator has boundaries whose expansions are definitionally identical old proof tracks. The actual association/MAC critical square is derived from it and proof-groupoid laws.",
    "For every stage n and typed continuation t, the constructed critical cell uses twelve higher syntax constructors and has support exactly {n,n+1} union t.needs. Constructor counts exclude boundary-track, circuit, wire, requirement and library payloads.",
    "Generic dependency accounting for explicitly supplied higher generators includes intermediate proof tracks and derives updated-kernel repair, including arbitrary correlated past data and original input.",
    "The critical-cell repair theorem requires only changed members of its declared support, cached old conditions, transfer outside the changed set, and an updated checked old-catalogue model."
  ],
